# Review rules for the platform.
#
# Enforced by branch protection on main: a pull request touching a path below
# cannot be merged without approval from its owner. This file plus branch
# protection is what turns "a commit deploys to production" into a controlled
# operation rather than an accident waiting to happen.
#
# See RUNBOOK.md, "Protección de la rama main", for the exact settings this
# file depends on. Without them, everything here is a suggestion.

# Default owner for anything not matched below.
*                           @morbanjunior

# Image tags: every deployment is a pull request that changes one of these
# files. staging and prod are promoted deliberately and need their owner.
apps/*/envs/staging.yaml    @morbanjunior
apps/*/envs/prod.yaml       @morbanjunior

# What exists in the cluster at all, and what each application is allowed to
# do. platform/projects/ is the isolation between applications: widening a rule
# there is a security change, not a configuration change.
bootstrap/                  @morbanjunior
platform/                   @morbanjunior

# The shared chart. One edit here changes how every application in every
# environment is rendered, which makes it the highest-blast-radius directory in
# the repository.
charts/                     @morbanjunior

# How image tags get proposed in the first place. A change here could widen
# what Renovate is allowed to touch beyond dev, or turn off the check that
# stands in for a reviewer there.
renovate.json5              @morbanjunior
.github/                    @morbanjunior

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately UNOWNED. Must stay the last rule in the file: in CODEOWNERS the
# last matching pattern wins, and a pattern with no owners clears ownership.
#
# dev is the one environment that updates without a human. Renovate opens the
# pull request and GitHub merges it once Validate is green -- the rendered
# manifests are the reviewer. Leaving @morbanjunior as owner here (via the
# catch-all at the top) would mean every dev deployment waits on a person to
# approve a two-line tag bump, which is the friction this repository is trying
# to spend where it matters: staging and production.
#
# The scope is narrow on purpose. This is the ONLY path Renovate can write to
# (see renovate.json5) and the only one that merges unattended. Everything else
# in apps/ is still covered by the catch-all.
apps/*/envs/dev.yaml
